<h2 id="experience">Experience</h2>

<h3 class="experience-header" id="amazon-web-services--8-years">Amazon Web Services — 8 years</h3>

<p>I build query languages, compliance frameworks, and autonomous engineering systems at AWS scale. Three threads run through my work: <strong>making complex systems accessible through language design</strong>, <strong>shifting enforcement left into infrastructure</strong>, and <strong>applying AI agents to accelerate the entire development lifecycle</strong>.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Observability Query Language &amp; Compiler Design</strong> — CloudWatch Logs (2022–present)</p>

<p>I own feature development for ScrollQL, the query compiler powering CloudWatch Logs Insights — the primary log analysis surface for millions of AWS customers. ScrollQL compiles a SQL-like query language into execution plans over distributed log storage; I work across the full compiler stack from parser extensions through AST rewrite rules to execution operators. I designed and shipped Lookup Tables end-to-end across 32 AWS regions — a query-time enrichment primitive that performs hash-join lookups against customer-provided reference data, enabling log enrichment (IP→hostname, error code→description) without data movement. I build aggregation functions (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">rate</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">avg</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sum</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">count</code>) that operate over streaming window semantics, and macro rewrite rules that expand high-level query patterns into optimized multi-stage execution plans. Feature delivery requires a 3-layer wiring chain: compile-time feature gates in the symbol table, a runtime bridge through the execution config provider, and region-level rollout via feature access control configuration.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Autonomous Software Engineering Systems</strong> (2025–present)</p>

<p>I designed Forge — a self-correcting autonomous pipeline where LLM agents take a feature specification and produce a shipped, reviewed code change with zero manual intervention. The architecture is a 5-phase state machine: workspace provisioning from the latest green pipeline event → compiler implementation via domain-specialized agents → test generation and validation → code review submission with structured descriptions → iterative watch loop that polls for static analysis findings, spawns repair agents, and pushes new revisions until clean. The system uses async job queuing, idempotent cron-driven state transitions (tick-based executor), tmux-isolated agent sessions for crash resilience, and a human-in-the-loop feedback path that detects reviewer comments and pauses autonomy until resolved. First end-to-end run delivered a production ScrollQL aggregation function in under 5 hours across 2 revisions and 34 watch polls. This work directly informs my research on multi-agent system observability.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Infrastructure Compliance at Scale</strong> — AWS CloudFormation (2018–2022)</p>

<p>I designed and built the Hooks control plane service — the API layer, invocation engine, and execution model for CloudFormation Hooks. Hooks intercept stack operations (create, update, delete) at the resource level and invoke customer-defined compliance handlers before provisioning proceeds, enabling policy-as-code enforcement (e.g., “no public S3 buckets”, “all EC2 instances must be tagged”) without modifying templates. I led a team of 6 engineers to deliver Hooks to GA, led the integration of Hooks as a new resource type into the existing CloudFormation Registry, and drove both systems across all AWS partitions (commercial, GovCloud, China, isolated regions). The resource provider framework I contributed to defines the contract between CloudFormation and hundreds of AWS service teams that implement resource handlers. This work shaped my thinking on how to build extensible, policy-driven systems that operate reliably across trust boundaries and partition isolation requirements.</p>

